  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "name": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore students generate anything, they make predictions: given a prompt for a gourmet pizza restaurant website, what sections will probably appear? Then they see the AI's output and compare it to what they guessed. That prediction moment matters — it gets students thinking about AI as a pattern-matching system rather than a magic box, which is exactly the framing the lesson then formalizes. Students learn how AI uses training data to recognize patterns and generate predictions, get introduced to prompt engineering, and see for the first time that their HTML and CSS live in two separate files. The gap between a vague prompt and a specific one is made concrete through side-by-side comparisons before students try it themselves.",
        "student_overview": "**How can AI help us turn our ideas into a web page?**\n\nBefore you build anything today, you're going to make a prediction. If you gave AI this prompt — \"Build a modern landing page for a gourmet pizza restaurant with a red and charcoal color palette\" — what do you think would show up? Make your guess, then find out if you were right. What you discover will tell you a lot about how AI actually works: not magic, not random, but pattern recognition. That's the foundation everything else in this unit is built on."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Build Your First Web Page with AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nThe lesson opens with a before-and-after comparison of two pizza ordering websites — one confusing, one improved — and asks students to notice what changed and what could go wrong if no one fixed the first version. That \"what could go wrong\" question is the lesson's moral center: accessibility failures aren't just aesthetic problems, they're barriers that determine whether someone can participate at all. Students work as Inclusive Designers, starting with the striking statistic that nearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues, running audits on Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA, and then learning that AI reproduces these patterns by default because it learns from the web that already exists. The POUR framework gives students a professional evaluation tool they apply to real-world outputs.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the Accessibility and Design activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How can we make sites usable for everyone?**\n\nNearly 95% of websites have accessibility issues. That's not a small problem — that's most of the internet failing a significant portion of its users. Today you're going to see that up close. You'll audit three well-known websites — Yale School of Art, MIT Media Lab, and NASA — using a professional framework called POUR, and you'll find real problems on real sites. Then you'll connect what you found to AI: where do those problems come from, and what does it mean that AI learned from websites like these?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Accessibility in Design",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "name": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nThe lesson's warm-up poses a simple question: would you rather have a map, written directions, or both? The answer is both — and that's exactly what students give AI in this lesson: a visual wireframe and a written description, together. Students see how AI interprets wireframes by detecting spacing, labels, grouping, and alignment, and why it gets things wrong when any of those are ambiguous. The lesson's central activity is the gap between what students intended their wireframe to communicate and what AI actually produced — and what they need to change in their prompt to close that gap. That iteration loop is the lesson's most important learning moment.",
        "preparation": "- Print copies of the From Wireframes to Layouts activity guide (one for each student)",
        "student_overview": "**How do we turn sketches into working layouts with AI?**\n\nWould you rather navigate somewhere with a map, written directions, or both? Both, right? Today you're going to give AI both: your wireframe for the visual layout and a written prompt for the context and content. Then you'll see what it does with them — and it might not be exactly what you expected. Your job is to figure out why AI interpreted your design the way it did and what you need to change to get it closer to what you actually had in mind. The clearer your wireframe and prompt, the closer the output will be. This is where planning pays off."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 11: From Wireframes to Layouts",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "<i class=\"fa-solid fa-user-graduate\"></i> ***Career Journeys***\n\n**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nStudents write their first event handler. A kinesthetic role-play — Element, User, Listener, Handler — grounds the vocabulary before the JavaScript appears on screen. This is also the unit's first explicit AI literacy moment: \"AI Confidence ≠ AI Correctness\" and \"Context Blindness\" are named, with a Show / Describe / Confirm debugging protocol when AI gives students code that doesn't fit their page. Front-End Developer enters as a real role through Maya's day-in-the-life chatbot interview.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we use JavaScript to make our websites more interactive?**\n\nA button isn't just a button — something is listening for your click, and something else handles what happens next. You learn the difference between an event listener and an event handler, write your first JavaScript that makes a page respond, and meet Maya, a Front-End Developer who builds this kind of thing every day. You also see your first moment of an AI giving you confident, wrong code — and learn how to catch it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: Making a Button Respond",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "name": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nStudents learn arrow functions, named vs. anonymous syntax, and abstraction — with the framing that clean, well-named code helps humans and AI work with shared code. The spec.md activity introduces literal [Specify] and [Predict] tags, the unit's first explicit handoff from natural-language planning to AI-generated code. \"Write Rewind on your own\" is the unit's first declared Write First task — AI Tutor will not write it.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my code cleaner and easier to reuse?**\n\nRepeating the same lines of code over and over gets messy fast. Functions let you write a chunk of code once and reuse it everywhere. You learn to define and call arrow functions, why naming matters (for you, your teammates, and the AI tools you work with), and how to write a quick plan in `spec.md` before you ask AI to generate code."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Writing Reusable Functions",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nStudents meet flowcharts and conditional statements together. The lesson names computer vision as the AI capability that lets students hand a sketched flowchart to AI as a visual spec — connecting visual planning directly to code generation. The Warm Up plants a Domain 4 hook (\"Who decided that liking one video means showing similar ones?\"), opening a conversation that returns in later lessons.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I make my app make choices based on user actions?**\n\nApps make decisions all day — show this if logged in, otherwise show that. The tool for planning those decisions is a flowchart, and the code that runs them is the `if/else` statement. You sketch your first flowchart, hand it to AI as a visual spec, and check whether the code AI writes actually follows your plan. You also start asking a bigger question: who decided the rules the apps you use follow?"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Making Decisions with If/Else",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "aif-advisory-council-preview"
      }
    }
  ],
